n reactor, and other low-output facilities can never be sufficient for the next generation of Tokamaks [4]. This issue is compounded by the potential deprecation of the CANDU reactors over the next decades, creating a low global inventory of tritium [5], even for start-up applications within the burgeoning fusion exosys- tem. A realistic reactor start-up may be infeasible by DEMO’s planned initialisation in the mid-2050 s unless large strides are made to improve breeder blanket efficiencies in near-term pro- totype fusion devices [5]. Additional pressure on the tritium supply chain arises from the private investment sector which is continually increasing the number of planned fusion reactors set to come online in the coming decades. This trend further increases the risk that tritium shortages will limit the develop- ment of fusion reactors and, worse, delay or cancel existing ignition timelines. In such a case, the community may need to turn to D-D reactions to pioneer an international fusion energy supply network [6]. 1.1. Current blanket designs Systems that conserve the total amount of tritium throughout the fuel cycle were quickly recognised in the early develop- ments of fusion [7]. Furthermore, blanket concepts of lith- ium breeders have been discussed since the

